Because .blend and .fbx files are binary formats, their internal data—including specific object coordinates—is not stored as human-readable text. As an AI, I cannot "see" the 3D viewport or parse binary 3D scene graphs directly to list every coordinate.

However, I can provide an extremely detailed breakdown of what is contained in each file based on their metadata and headers, and I will give you the Python script you requested to run inside Blender. This script will automatically generate the exact list of every asset, its location, rotation, and size (scale) that you need.

1. Detailed Breakdown of the Files
Based on the file contents:

pickels.blend: This is your primary Blender scene file.


Scene Name: "TEST".


Color Management: Uses Linear Rec.709.

Content: Contains the full 3D database including meshes, lighting, and workspace settings.

pickels.blend1: This is a backup file automatically generated by Blender. It is a snapshot of your project from the previous save.


pickels.fbx: This is a binary FBX file exported from Unity Technologies using FBX SDK 2020.3.4.


Included Assets: Nodes, Meshes, Materials, Textures, Cameras, Lights, and Animation Skins.


Animation Data: Contains multiple animation takes, specifically one named "SpinFan".


Coordinate System: Likely uses Unity’s Y-up or Z-up depending on export settings, with properties for Lcl Translation, Lcl Rotation, and Lcl Scaling.


pickels.fbx.meta: A Unity metadata file.


GUID: a2c59048f3002a64c85ce79216b777fd.


Settings: Contains import instructions for Unity, such as animation compression, mesh optimization, and material location (set to 1, meaning it looks for external materials).

2. The Script to Extract All Asset Locations
To get the exact list of every object’s location and size as you requested, you can use the script below. You can run this in Visual Studio Code (if connected to Blender) or directly in Blender's Scripting tab.

Python
import bpy

# Create a new text file to store the data
output_file = "scene_assets_detail.txt"
file_path = bpy.path.abspath("//" + output_file)

with open(file_path, 'w') as f:
    f.write("BLENDER SCENE ASSET BREAKDOWN\n")
    f.write("="*30 + "\n\n")

    # Iterate through every object in the scene
    for obj in bpy.data.objects:
        f.write(f"NAME: {obj.name}\n")
        f.write(f"TYPE: {obj.type}\n")
        
        # Get Location (XYZ)
        loc = obj.location
        f.write(f"LOCATION: X: {loc.x:.4f}, Y: {loc.y:.4f}, Z: {loc.z:.4f}\n")
        
        # Get Rotation (Euler)
        rot = obj.rotation_euler
        f.write(f"ROTATION: X: {rot.x:.4f}, Y: {rot.y:.4f}, Z: {rot.z:.4f}\n")
        
        # Get Scale/Size
        scale = obj.scale
        f.write(f"SCALE (SIZE): X: {scale.x:.4f}, Y: {scale.y:.4f}, Z: {scale.z:.4f}\n")
        
        # Check for associated data (Mesh info)
        if obj.type == 'MESH':
            f.write(f"VERTICES: {len(obj.data.vertices)}\n")
            f.write(f"POLYGONS: {len(obj.data.polygons)}\n")
        
        # Check for scripts/custom properties
        if obj.keys():
            f.write("CUSTOM PROPERTIES/SCRIPTS: " + ", ".join(obj.keys()) + "\n")
            
        f.write("-" * 20 + "\n")

print(f"Detail list generated! Look for '{output_file}' in your project folder.")
How to use this:
In Blender: Go to the Scripting workspace, click New, paste the code, and hit Run Script.

In VS Code: If you have the "Blender Development" extension, use the Blender: Run Script command.

The Result: This will create a text file named scene_assets_detail.txt in the same folder as your .blend file. It will contain a line-by-line list of every asset, its exact coordinates, and its size.

