Because .blend files are binary, PowerShell cannot read the object coordinates directly from the file itself. To get that data, PowerShell must "ask" Blender to open the file in the background and report back.

This PowerShell script creates a bridge: it launches a headless (no interface) instance of Blender, runs a Python command to gather all object locations and sizes, and then displays the results in your console.

