This PowerShell script automates the process of opening your pickels.blend file in the background (headless mode) using Blender's command-line interface, running the Python logic you provided, and outputting the results to a text file.

PowerShell Automation Script
Save this code as a file named ExtractBlenderData.ps1 or run it directly in your PowerShell terminal inside Visual Studio Code.

PowerShell
# 1. Path to your Blender executable
# Change this if your Blender is installed in a different location
$BlenderExe = "C:\Program Files\Blender Foundation\Blender 4.0\blender.exe"

# 2. Path to your specific blend file
$BlendFile = "pickels.blend"

# 3. Define the Python logic to execute inside Blender
$PythonCode = @"
import bpy
import os

# Create the output report file
output_file = 'scene_assets_detail.txt'
file_path = os.path.abspath(output_file)

with open(file_path, 'w') as f:
    f.write('=== EXTREMELY DETAILED ASSET BREAKDOWN ===\n')
    f.write(f'Source File: {bpy.data.filepath}\n')
    f.write('='*40 + '\n\n')

    # 1. Analyze Objects
    f.write('[OBJECT LOCATIONS AND DIMENSIONS]\n')
    for obj in bpy.data.objects:
        f.write(f'NAME: {obj.name}\n')
        f.write(f'  - TYPE: {obj.type}\n')
        f.write(f'  - LOCATION: X: {obj.location.x:.6f}, Y: {obj.location.y:.6f}, Z: {obj.location.z:.6f}\n')
        f.write(f'  - ROTATION: X: {obj.rotation_euler.x:.6f}, Y: {obj.rotation_euler.y:.6f}, Z: {obj.rotation_euler.z:.6f}\n')
        f.write(f'  - SCALE:    X: {obj.scale.x:.6f}, Y: {obj.scale.y:.6f}, Z: {obj.scale.z:.6f}\n')
        
        if obj.type == 'MESH':
            f.write(f'  - MESH DATA: Verts: {len(obj.data.vertices)}, Polys: {len(obj.data.polygons)}\n')
        
        if obj.keys():
            f.write(f'  - CUSTOM DATA/SCRIPTS: {", ".join(obj.keys())}\n')
        f.write('-' * 20 + '\n')

    # 2. Analyze Internal Scripts/Texts
    f.write('\n[INTERNAL SCRIPTS AND TEXT FILES]\n')
    if not bpy.data.texts:
        f.write('No internal scripts found.\n')
    for text in bpy.data.texts:
        f.write(f'SCRIPT NAME: {text.name}\n')
        f.write('--- START CONTENT ---\n')
        f.write(text.as_string())
        f.write('\n--- END CONTENT ---\n')

print(f'Full detail report generated at: {file_path}')
"@

# 4. Save the Python logic to a temp file for Blender to read
$TempPy = "temp_extractor.py"
$PythonCode | Out-File -FilePath $TempPy -Encoding utf8

# 5. Execute Blender in Background Mode
Write-Host "Starting Blender extraction for $BlendFile..." -ForegroundColor Cyan
& $BlenderExe -b $BlendFile -P $TempPy

# 6. Cleanup the temporary Python script
if (Test-Path $TempPy) { Remove-Item $TempPy }

Write-Host "Done! Please open 'scene_assets_detail.txt' to see the full coordinate list." -ForegroundColor Green
How to use this:
Check the Path: Ensure $BlenderExe points to where blender.exe is actually installed on your PC.

Run in VS Code: Open the PowerShell terminal in VS Code (Ctrl + `) and run the script.

The Result: It will generate a file called scene_assets_detail.txt. This file contains the exact coordinates, rotations, and scales for every object, as well as the content of any scripts inside the .blend file.

Prompt for the Gemini Extension in VS Code
If you want to use the Gemini program inside Visual Studio Code to handle this or future modifications, use this prompt:

"I am working with a Blender project containing four files: pickels.blend, pickels.blend1, pickels.fbx, and pickels.fbx.meta. I need a Python script that uses the bpy module to crawl through the entire scene.

For every object, I need its absolute world-space coordinates, Euler rotation, and local scale. I also need you to check for any internal Python text blocks (scripts) and print their contents. Output all this information into a structured text file so I can use it to rebuild the scene programmatically.

Additionally, since some data is in the FBX file, provide a logic block that imports the FBX and lists its hierarchy and animation takes (like 'SpinFan'). Format the output so every line includes the object name and its location data for easy parsing."

Breakdown of All Four Files
pickels.blend: The main database. This contains your 3D models, their materials, and the world settings (Scene: "TEST").

pickels.blend1: This is a direct backup of the .blend file. If the main file ever gets corrupted, you can rename this to .blend to recover your work.

pickels.fbx: A portable 3D file exported from Unity. It contains an animation called "SpinFan". It is used to move the 3D data between Blender and game engines like Unity or Unreal.

pickels.fbx.meta: This is a Unity-specific file. It tells the Unity Editor how to import the FBX (scale, mesh optimization, and animation settings). It confirms the file has a GUID of a2c59048f3002a64c85ce79216b777fd.

