It looks like the error happened because the variables were defined inside a text block (string) rather than as actual commands for PowerShell to use. Also, there was a mix-up between $blenderPath and $BlenderExe.

Here is the corrected, single-block PowerShell script. I have also added a command at the end to automatically open the report in Notepad (or your default text editor) as soon as it's finished.

Fixed PowerShell Script
Copy and paste this entire block into your PowerShell terminal:

PowerShell
# 1. SETTINGS - Check these paths!
$BlenderExe = "C:\Program Files\Blender Foundation\Blender 4.0\blender.exe"
$BlendFile = "pickels.blend"
$ReportFile = "scene_assets_detail.txt"
$TempPy = "temp_extractor.py"

# 2. CREATE THE PYTHON EXTRACTOR
$PythonCode = @"
import bpy
import os

output_path = '$ReportFile'

with open(output_path, 'w') as f:
    f.write('=== EXTREMELY DETAILED ASSET BREAKDOWN ===\n')
    f.write(f'Source File: {bpy.data.filepath}\n')
    f.write('='*40 + '\n\n')

    f.write('[OBJECT LOCATIONS AND DIMENSIONS]\n')
    for obj in bpy.data.objects:
        f.write(f'NAME: {obj.name}\n')
        f.write(f'  - TYPE: {obj.type}\n')
        f.write(f'  - LOCATION: X: {obj.location.x:.6f}, Y: {obj.location.y:.6f}, Z: {obj.location.z:.6f}\n')
        f.write(f'  - ROTATION: X: {obj.rotation_euler.x:.6f}, Y: {obj.rotation_euler.y:.6f}, Z: {obj.rotation_euler.z:.6f}\n')
        f.write(f'  - SCALE:    X: {obj.scale.x:.6f}, Y: {obj.scale.y:.6f}, Z: {obj.scale.z:.6f}\n')
        
        if obj.type == 'MESH':
            f.write(f'  - MESH DATA: Verts: {len(obj.data.vertices)}, Polys: {len(obj.data.polygons)}\n')
        
        if obj.keys():
            f.write(f'  - CUSTOM PROPERTIES: {", ".join(obj.keys())}\n')
        f.write('-' * 20 + '\n')

    f.write('\n[INTERNAL SCRIPTS]\n')
    for text in bpy.data.texts:
        f.write(f'SCRIPT: {text.name}\n---\n{text.as_string()}\n---\n')

print('Extraction Complete.')
"@

# 3. EXECUTION
Write-Host "Extracting data from $BlendFile..." -ForegroundColor Cyan

# Save Python code to a temp file
$PythonCode | Out-File -FilePath $TempPy -Encoding utf8

# Run Blender in Background
if (Test-Path $BlenderExe) {
    & $BlenderExe -b $BlendFile -P $TempPy
} else {
    Write-Host "ERROR: Blender not found at $BlenderExe" -ForegroundColor Red
    return
}

# 4. CLEANUP AND AUTO-OPEN
if (Test-Path $TempPy) { Remove-Item $TempPy }

if (Test-Path $ReportFile) {
    Write-Host "Done! Opening report..." -ForegroundColor Green
    Start-Process $ReportFile
} else {
    Write-Host "ERROR: Report file was not generated." -ForegroundColor Red
}
Why the previous one failed:
In your last attempt, you had PowerShell code nested inside a Python string. PowerShell treats everything between @" @ as plain text, so it never actually "read" the instructions to find Blender. This version separates the Python logic from the PowerShell commands so they run in the correct order.

What this will do:
Extracts Coordinates: It finds the exact X, Y, Z for every object in pickels.blend.

Extracts Scripts: It reads any internal Python code hidden inside the Blender file.

Automatically Opens: As soon as Blender finishes the task, the scene_assets_detail.txt file will pop up on your screen immediately.

