The error occurred because Blender is not installed in the default "4.0" folder on your computer. You might have version 3.6, 4.1, or 4.2 installed, or it might be in a different location.

Here is a Universal PowerShell Script that will automatically search your computer for Blender, run the extraction on your pickels.blend file, and then pop open the report immediately.

The "Find and Extract" PowerShell Script
PowerShell
# 1. AUTO-FIND BLENDER (Searches common locations)
$PossiblePaths = @(
    "C:\Program Files\Blender Foundation\Blender 4.2\blender.exe",
    "C:\Program Files\Blender Foundation\Blender 4.1\blender.exe",
    "C:\Program Files\Blender Foundation\Blender 4.0\blender.exe",
    "C:\Program Files\Blender Foundation\Blender 3.6\blender.exe",
    "$env:PROGRAMFILES\Blender Foundation\*\blender.exe"
)

$BlenderExe = $PossiblePaths | Resolve-Path -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | Select-Object -ExpandProperty Path -First 1

if (-not $BlenderExe) {
    Write-Host "CRITICAL ERROR: Blender.exe could not be found automatically." -ForegroundColor Red
    Write-Host "Please find your blender.exe and paste the path here:" -ForegroundColor Yellow
    $BlenderExe = Read-Host "Path"
}

# 2. FILE SETTINGS
$BlendFile = "pickels.blend"
$ReportFile = "scene_assets_detail.txt"
$TempPy = "temp_extractor.py"

# 3. DEFINE THE EXTRACTION LOGIC
$PythonCode = @"
import bpy
import os

output_path = '$ReportFile'

with open(output_path, 'w') as f:
    f.write('=== EXTREMELY DETAILED ASSET BREAKDOWN ===\n')
    f.write('SOURCE: ' + bpy.data.filepath + '\n')
    f.write('='*40 + '\n\n')

    # Breakdown of every object
    f.write('[OBJECT HIERARCHY & COORDINATES]\n')
    for obj in bpy.data.objects:
        f.write(f'ASSET NAME: {obj.name}\n')
        f.write(f'  - TYPE:     {obj.type}\n')
        f.write(f'  - POSITION: X: {obj.location.x:.6f}, Y: {obj.location.y:.6f}, Z: {obj.location.z:.6f}\n')
        f.write(f'  - ROTATION: X: {obj.rotation_euler.x:.6f}, Y: {obj.rotation_euler.y:.6f}, Z: {obj.rotation_euler.z:.6f}\n')
        f.write(f'  - SCALE:    X: {obj.scale.x:.6f}, Y: {obj.scale.y:.6f}, Z: {obj.scale.z:.6f}\n')
        
        if obj.type == 'MESH':
            f.write(f'  - GEOMETRY: {len(obj.data.vertices)} Verts, {len(obj.data.polygons)} Polys\n')
        
        if obj.parent:
            f.write(f'  - PARENTED TO: {obj.parent.name}\n')
            
        f.write('-' * 30 + '\n')

    # Breakdown of every script/text block
    f.write('\n[INTERNAL CODE & SCRIPTS]\n')
    if not bpy.data.texts:
        f.write('No internal Python scripts found.\n')
    else:
        for text in bpy.data.texts:
            f.write(f'SCRIPT: {text.name}\n' + '-'*10 + '\n')
            f.write(text.as_string() + '\n' + '-'*10 + '\n')

print('Extraction Complete.')
"@

# 4. EXECUTE
Write-Host "Using Blender at: $BlenderExe" -ForegroundColor Cyan
$PythonCode | Out-File -FilePath $TempPy -Encoding utf8

# Run Blender in background mode
& $BlenderExe -b $BlendFile -P $TempPy

# 5. CLEANUP AND OPEN
if (Test-Path $TempPy) { Remove-Item $TempPy }

if (Test-Path $ReportFile) {
    Write-Host "Success! Opening full asset breakdown now..." -ForegroundColor Green
    Start-Process notepad.exe $ReportFile
} else {
    Write-Host "Error: Could not generate report. Check if '$BlendFile' exists in this folder." -ForegroundColor Red
}
How to run this successfully:
Open VS Code.

Make sure you are in the folder where pickels.blend is located.

Paste the script above into your PowerShell terminal.

Wait for the popup: Blender will run invisibly for a few seconds, then Notepad will automatically open with every coordinate, asset name, and script content listed line-by-line.

Why this is better:
Auto-Detection: It looks for any version of Blender (3.6 to 4.2) so you don't have to manually edit the version number.

Internal Script Extraction: If there are hidden "Z scripts" or Python scripts inside the .blend file, this will pull the raw code out and put it in the text file for you.

Coordinate Precision: It outputs coordinates to 6 decimal places, which is exactly what you need for "memory training" or programmatic recreation.

Breakdown of the 4 Files (Final Summary)
pickels.blend: Your master database. Holds the objects, locations, and "TEST" scene.

pickels.blend1: An emergency recovery file.

pickels.fbx: A snapshot of the model used for Unity. It specifically contains the SpinFan animation.

pickels.fbx.meta: The Unity "brain" for the FBX, which stores the GUID and import settings.

