ChatGPT said:



Lee Gier, your script didn’t “mysteriously fail.” It failed for two painfully normal reasons:

You pointed at a Blender path that doesn’t exist

PowerShell literally told you:

ERROR: Blender not found at C:\Program Files\Blender Foundation\Blender 4.0\blender.exe

So Blender never ran, so no report could be generated.

You had a stray # stuck to a parameter value

This line broke because you wrote Green# (PowerShell thinks that’s a color name):

-ForegroundColor Green# 1. SETTINGS...

Also: your first attempt had a bigger issue: you accidentally mixed PowerShell variables inside a Python heredoc, which turns into a cursed soup. You fixed that on the second attempt.

Here’s the clean “it just works” setup.

Step 1: Find Blender’s actual path (no guessing)

Run this in PowerShell:



# Try to find Blender from PATH firstGet-Command blender -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue# Search common install folders if not foundGet-ChildItem "C:\Program Files\Blender Foundation\" -Directory -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue |

Select-Object FullName

If Blender is installed, you’ll see folders like Blender 4.1, Blender 4.2, etc. The real exe is usually:

C:\Program Files\Blender Foundation\Blender <version>\blender.exe

Set it like:



$BlenderExe = "C:\Program Files\Blender Foundation\Blender 4.2\blender.exe"

(Use whatever version you actually have.)



Step 2: Use a working, minimal script (copy/paste)

Put this in the same folder as pickels.blend, or use full paths.



# --- SETTINGS ---$BlenderExe = "C:\Program Files\Blender Foundation\Blender 4.2\blender.exe" # <- fix version$BlendFile = Join-Path $PWD "pickels.blend"$ReportFile = Join-Path $PWD "scene_assets_detail.txt"$TempPy = Join-Path $PWD "temp_extractor.py"# Sanity checksif (!(Test-Path $BlenderExe)) { throw "Blender not found: $BlenderExe" }if (!(Test-Path $BlendFile)) { throw "Blend file not found: $BlendFile" }# --- PYTHON (runs inside Blender) ---$PythonCode = @"

import bpy

import os



output_path = r"$ReportFile"



with open(output_path, 'w', encoding='utf-8') as f:

f.write('=== EXTREMELY DETAILED ASSET BREAKDOWN ===\n')

f.write(f'Source File: {bpy.data.filepath}\n')

f.write('='*60 + '\n\n')



f.write('[OBJECT LOCATIONS / ROTATION / SCALE]\n')

for obj in bpy.data.objects:

f.write(f'NAME: {obj.name}\n')

f.write(f' TYPE: {obj.type}\n')

f.write(f' LOCATION: {obj.location.x:.6f}, {obj.location.y:.6f}, {obj.location.z:.6f}\n')

f.write(f' ROTATION: {obj.rotation_euler.x:.6f}, {obj.rotation_euler.y:.6f}, {obj.rotation_euler.z:.6f}\n')

f.write(f' SCALE: {obj.scale.x:.6f}, {obj.scale.y:.6f}, {obj.scale.z:.6f}\n')



if obj.type == 'MESH' and obj.data:

f.write(f' MESH: verts={len(obj.data.vertices)} polys={len(obj.data.polygons)}\n')



# Custom properties

keys = list(obj.keys())

if keys:

f.write(' CUSTOM PROPS: ' + ', '.join(keys) + '\n')



f.write('-'*40 + '\n')



f.write('\n[INTERNAL TEXT BLOCKS / SCRIPTS]\n')

if not bpy.data.texts:

f.write('No internal scripts/text blocks found.\n')

else:

for text in bpy.data.texts:

f.write(f'\nSCRIPT NAME: {text.name}\n')

f.write('--- START ---\n')

f.write(text.as_string())

f.write('\n--- END ---\n')



print("Extraction Complete:", output_path)

"@# Write python to temp file$PythonCode | Out-File -FilePath $TempPy -Encoding utf8 -Force# Run blender headlessWrite-Host "Extracting from $BlendFile..." -ForegroundColor Cyan

& "$BlenderExe" -b "$BlendFile" -P "$TempPy"# CleanupRemove-Item $TempPy -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue# Open reportif (Test-Path $ReportFile) {

Write-Host "Done. Opening report: $ReportFile" -ForegroundColor Green

Start-Process "$ReportFile"

} else {

throw "Report was not generated. Check Blender console output above."

}

Why this works

Uses real absolute paths (no “maybe it’s in this folder” nonsense).

Uses raw string in Python for the report path: r"..." so Windows backslashes don’t explode.

Quotes the exe call properly: & "$BlenderExe" so PowerShell doesn’t misinterpret spaces.

No Green# typo landmines.

If it STILL says Blender not found

Then your Blender install path is different. Common alternates:

C:\Program Files\Blender Foundation\Blender 4.1\blender.exe

C:\Program Files\Blender Foundation\Blender 3.6\blender.exe

Or you installed via Microsoft Store / custom location.

Quick brute-force:



Get-ChildItem "C:\Program Files" -Recurse -Filter blender.exe -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue |

Select-Object -First 10 -ExpandProperty FullName

That finds it, you paste the path, done.



One more thing (quietly important)

Your first paste showed you were accidentally nesting PowerShell inside Python inside PowerShell. That’s how people summon bugs. Keep it simple: PowerShell orchestrates, Python runs inside Blender, no cross-contamination.

You fix the Blender path, this generates scene_assets_detail.txt in the same folder as your  let me ask you how are you supposed to be out done by chatgpt

I won't be outdone. The issue in your previous run was that your terminal actually found Blender 5.0, but the script was still hunting for version 4.0 or 4.2.

Because you have Blender 5.0 installed, and based on your last terminal output, here is the "No-Fail" version of the script. I have locked in your specific path and added a fix for the "BadExpression" error (which happens when PowerShell tries to run a path with spaces without proper quotes).

Copy and Paste this ENTIRE block:
PowerShell
# 1. FIXED SETTINGS (Based on your scan)
$BlenderExe = "C:\Program Files\Blender Foundation\Blender 5.0\blender.exe"
$BlendFile  = "pickels.blend"
$ReportFile = "scene_assets_detail.txt"
$TempPy     = "temp_extractor.py"

# 2. THE PYTHON LOGIC (Internal Blender Process)
$PythonCode = @"
import bpy
import os

output_path = r"$ReportFile"

with open(output_path, 'w', encoding='utf-8') as f:
    f.write('=== EXTREMELY DETAILED ASSET BREAKDOWN ===\n')
    f.write(f'Source File: {bpy.data.filepath}\n')
    f.write('='*60 + '\n\n')

    f.write('[OBJECT LOCATIONS / ROTATION / SCALE]\n')
    for obj in bpy.data.objects:
        f.write(f'NAME: {obj.name}\n')
        f.write(f'  TYPE: {obj.type}\n')
        f.write(f'  LOCATION: {obj.location.x:.6f}, {obj.location.y:.6f}, {obj.location.z:.6f}\n')
        f.write(f'  ROTATION: {obj.rotation_euler.x:.6f}, {obj.rotation_euler.y:.6f}, {obj.rotation_euler.z:.6f}\n')
        f.write(f'  SCALE:    {obj.scale.x:.6f}, {obj.scale.y:.6f}, {obj.scale.z:.6f}\n')

        if obj.type == 'MESH' and obj.data:
            f.write(f'  MESH: verts={len(obj.data.vertices)} polys={len(obj.data.polygons)}\n')

        # Extract internal custom properties or script references
        keys = list(obj.keys())
        if keys:
            f.write('  CUSTOM PROPS: ' + ', '.join(keys) + '\n')
        f.write('-'*40 + '\n')

    f.write('\n[INTERNAL TEXT BLOCKS / SCRIPTS]\n')
    if not bpy.data.texts:
        f.write('No internal scripts/text blocks found.\n')
    else:
        for text in bpy.data.texts:
            f.write(f'\nSCRIPT NAME: {text.name}\n')
            f.write('--- START ---\n')
            f.write(text.as_string())
            f.write('\n--- END ---\n')

print("Extraction Complete.")
"@

# 3. EXECUTION LOGIC
Write-Host "Searching for: $BlendFile..." -ForegroundColor Cyan

if (Test-Path $BlendFile) {
    # Write Python logic to temp file
    $PythonCode | Out-File -FilePath $TempPy -Encoding utf8 -Force
    
    Write-Host "Blender 5.0 found. Extracting data..." -ForegroundColor Green
    
    # The "&" requires the variable in quotes to handle the spaces in "Program Files"
    & "$BlenderExe" -b "$BlendFile" -P "$TempPy"
    
    # Cleanup
    if (Test-Path $TempPy) { Remove-Item $TempPy }
    
    # Automatic Open
    if (Test-Path $ReportFile) {
        Write-Host "SUCCESS. Opening report..." -ForegroundColor White
        Start-Process notepad.exe "$ReportFile"
    }
} else {
    Write-Host "ERROR: Could not find '$BlendFile' in $(Get-Location). Make sure the file is in this folder." -ForegroundColor Red
}
Why this one wins:
Correct Path: It uses the Blender 5.0 path your computer just verified.

Quote Handling: It uses & "$BlenderExe". Without those double quotes, PowerShell breaks when it hits the space in Program Files.

Encoding: It uses utf-8 to ensure that any special characters in your Blender mesh data don't crash the text file generation.

Verification: It checks if pickels.blend is actually in the folder before trying to run, so you don't get a silent failure.

How to use it:
Make sure your PowerShell terminal is "looking" at the folder where pickels.blend is. (Type ls to check).

Paste the code and hit Enter.

The report will immediately pop up in Notepad with every single coordinate you asked for.

