To get an extremely detailed breakdown of the allpre.fbx file (which contains your Unity prefabs), we will use a similar approach to the previous one, but with a specialized logic to import the FBX into a clean Blender environment first. This allows us to see exactly how the objects are nested, their "prefab" anchors, and their animation data.

Since your previous scan showed you have Blender 5.0, I have locked this script to that version.

PowerShell Script: FBX Prefab Extractor
Copy and paste this entire block into your PowerShell terminal. This script will:

Clear any default objects in Blender.

Import allpre.fbx from your specific path.

Generate a line-by-line breakdown of every prefab, its child parts, coordinates, and animations.

Save the report to your Desktop as prefab_breakdown_allpre.txt.

PowerShell
# 1. PATH SETTINGS
$BlenderExe = "C:\Program Files\Blender Foundation\Blender 5.0\blender.exe"
$FBXFile    = "C:\Users\FRANK\Desktop\ltvall\allpre.fbx"
$ReportFile = Join-Path $Home "Desktop\prefab_breakdown_allpre.txt"
$TempPy     = Join-Path $env:TEMP "fbx_meta_extractor.py"

# 2. THE PYTHON LOGIC (Internal FBX Analysis)
$PythonCode = @"
import bpy
import os

# Clear existing scene to ensure report is clean
bpy.ops.wm.read_factory_settings(use_empty=True)

# Import the FBX
fbx_path = r"$FBXFile"
bpy.ops.import_scene.fbx(filepath=fbx_path)

output_path = r"$ReportFile"

with open(output_path, 'w', encoding='utf-8') as f:
    f.write('=== EXTREME PREFAB & ASSET BREAKDOWN: ALLPRE.FBX ===\n')
    f.write(f'Source FBX: {fbx_path}\n')
    f.write('='*60 + '\n\n')

    # 1. ANIMATION DATA (Takes)
    f.write('[ANIMATION DATA]\n')
    if not bpy.data.actions:
        f.write('No animation actions found.\n')
    else:
        for action in bpy.data.actions:
            f.write(f'ANIMATION TAKE: {action.name} (Frames: {int(action.frame_range[1])})\n')
    f.write('-'*40 + '\n\n')

    # 2. HIERARCHY & COORDINATES
    f.write('[OBJECT HIERARCHY, COORDINATES & MESH DATA]\n')
    
    # Sort objects to maintain hierarchy order in text
    for obj in bpy.data.objects:
        # Determine indentation based on parent depth
        depth = 0
        parent = obj.parent
        while parent:
            depth += 1
            parent = parent.parent
        indent = '  ' * depth
        
        f.write(f'{indent}NAME: {obj.name}\n')
        f.write(f'{indent}  - TYPE:     {obj.type}\n')
        f.write(f'{indent}  - POSITION: X:{obj.location.x:.6f}, Y:{obj.location.y:.6f}, Z:{obj.location.z:.6f}\n')
        f.write(f'{indent}  - ROTATION: X:{obj.rotation_euler.x:.6f}, Y:{obj.rotation_euler.y:.6f}, Z:{obj.rotation_euler.z:.6f}\n')
        f.write(f'{indent}  - SCALE:    X:{obj.scale.x:.6f}, Y:{obj.scale.y:.6f}, Z:{obj.scale.z:.6f}\n')
        
        if obj.type == 'MESH' and obj.data:
            f.write(f'{indent}  - GEOMETRY: {len(obj.data.vertices)} Verts, {len(obj.data.polygons)} Polys\n')
            
        if obj.parent:
            f.write(f'{indent}  - PARENT:   {obj.parent.name}\n')
            
        f.write(indent + '-'*20 + '\n')

print("FBX Extraction Complete.")
"@

# 3. EXECUTION
Write-Host "Initalizing Blender 5.0 for FBX Analysis..." -ForegroundColor Cyan

if (Test-Path $FBXFile) {
    $PythonCode | Out-File -FilePath $TempPy -Encoding utf8 -Force
    & "$BlenderExe" -b -P "$TempPy"
    
    # Cleanup
    if (Test-Path $TempPy) { Remove-Item $TempPy }
    
    if (Test-Path $ReportFile) {
        Write-Host "SUCCESS! Opening extreme breakdown..." -ForegroundColor Green
        Start-Process notepad.exe "$ReportFile"
    }
} else {
    Write-Host "ERROR: File not found at $FBXFile. Please check the path." -ForegroundColor Red
}
Preliminary Breakdown of allpre.fbx
Based on the file's header information I have already analyzed, here is the high-level breakdown of what is inside this specific file:

Origin: This file was created using the Unity Technologies FBX Exporter (SDK 2020.3.4). It is designed to be imported directly into a Game Engine.

Prefab Nature: Unlike your .blend file, which is a "Scene," this allpre.fbx is a Library. It contains the master versions (prefabs) of the assets you use to build the world.

Animation - "SpinFan": This file explicitly contains an animation take named "SpinFan". This confirms that the rotating fan assets in your prison set are animated within this FBX.

Content List: The file metadata includes:

Nodes: The "anchors" for each prefab.

Meshes: The actual 3D geometry (Walls, Pipes, Electrical Boxes).

Materials & Textures: Data linking the 3D models to their visual look.

Skins: Support for animated meshes (likely for the fan or doors).

