The script failed because Conda is installed in a non-standard location or is not yet added to your system's environment variables. We need to find where conda.exe actually lives on your DEV-BOXX before we can activate the Nyghtshade environment.

Step 1: Locate your Conda Installation
Run this command in your PowerShell terminal to search your primary drive for the real path:

PowerShell
Get-ChildItem -Path "C:\" -Filter "conda.exe" -Recurse -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | Select-Object -First 1 -ExpandProperty FullName
Step 2: The "Titanium" Conda Initializer (Version 2.0)
Once you have the path from Step 1 (or if you know where you installed it), use this fixed script. I have corrected the "Purple" color error (PowerShell uses Magenta) and added a path-probing loop.

Copy and paste this ENTIRE block:

PowerShell
# 1. BRAXTIG PATH PROBE - Searching common root and user paths
$PossibleCondaPaths = @(
    "$env:USERPROFILE\anaconda3\Scripts\conda.exe",
    "$env:USERPROFILE\miniconda3\Scripts\conda.exe",
    "C:\ProgramData\anaconda3\Scripts\conda.exe",
    "C:\anaconda3\Scripts\conda.exe",
    "C:\miniconda3\Scripts\conda.exe",
    "C:\Users\$env:USERNAME\AppData\Local\anaconda3\Scripts\conda.exe",
    "C:\Users\$env:USERNAME\AppData\Local\miniconda3\Scripts\conda.exe"
)

$CondaExe = $null
foreach ($Path in $PossibleCondaPaths) {
    if (Test-Path $Path) { $CondaExe = $Path; break }
}

if (-not $CondaExe) {
    Write-Host "CRITICAL ERROR: Conda.exe not found automatically." -ForegroundColor Red
    Write-Host "Please find your 'Scripts' folder inside your Conda install and paste the path to conda.exe here:" -ForegroundColor Yellow
    $CondaExe = Read-Host "Full path to conda.exe"
}

# 2. INITIALIZE SHELL
Write-Host "BraxtigConfig: Initializing Conda from $CondaExe..." -ForegroundColor Cyan
& $CondaExe "shell.powershell" "hook" | Out-String | Invoke-Expression

# 3. ENVIRONMENT PROTOCOL
$EnvName = "nyghtshade_hollow"
$EnvList = conda env list
if ($EnvList -notmatch $EnvName) {
    Write-Host "Creating isolated environment: $EnvName..." -ForegroundColor Yellow
    conda create -n $EnvName python=3.9 -y
}

# 4. ACTIVATION
Write-Host "ACTIVATING PROTOCOL: $EnvName" -ForegroundColor Green
conda activate $EnvName

# 5. FINAL STATUS (Fixed Color)
Write-Host "Re-Volt GPT on Hit. Conda is live." -ForegroundColor Magenta
python --version
Why this version will work:
Deep Probing: It checks C:\ProgramData and AppData\Local, which are common spots for "All Users" or "Just Me" installations that the first script missed.

Manual Fallback: If it still can't find it, it stops and asks you to paste the path so we don't keep guessing.

PowerShell Compatibility: I swapped -ForegroundColor Purple for Magenta to prevent that syntax error you saw in the logs.

